# Example agreement-map (D3, issue #64) — a cross-paper agreement map at real-corpus grain
#
# Purpose: exhibit, in ONE multi-paper unit, the two outcomes the calculus distinguishes when
# papers in the same field appear to disagree — and show that the distinction is decided by ATOM
# IDENTITY (spec §3.2), not by prose. Example `B` proves the shape synthetically; this scales it
# to the pruning cluster's real vocabulary (`corpus-units/corpus-v1.policy.lara`, family 1) with
# two claim pairs side by side.
#
# The cluster: structured/unstructured pruning of transformer LMs — the four "papers" below are
# the pruning family the corpus already annotates (`corpus-units/adaptive-pruning/`, whose C03
# claim is exactly `better(apt, prune_distill_cofi, ...)`). Two propositions are hand-aligned:
#
#   P1 (genuine disagreement): APT vs Prune+Distill (CoFi), accuracy, RoBERTa-base/MNLI at 60%
#      sparsity. One paper reports APT superior; an independent replication reports no advantage.
#      Both formalize to the SAME (S, B, Q, D) atoms, so `better` / `not_better` are a declared
#      contrary instance -> mutual rebut -> a `contested` 2-cycle.
#
#   P2 (setting mismatch): magnitude pruning vs the dense baseline, accuracy. One paper concludes
#      pruning is (near-)lossless; another concludes pruning destroys accuracy. In prose this is a
#      flat contradiction. But they measured DIFFERENT settings — BERT/GLUE at 50% sparsity vs
#      LLaMA2-7B/OpenLLM at 90% sparsity — so they formalize to `better(mp, dense, accuracy,
#      bert_glue_s50)` and `not_better(mp, dense, accuracy, llama_openllm_s90)`. Same system,
#      baseline, and metric; the ONLY difference is the setting index D. The contrary pair
#      requires the SAME D, so it does not unify: ZERO attacks. Both claims stand justified.
#
# A3 (atom-matching) discipline, README.md design constraint #1: the alignment decision is
# recorded per proposition in the `binding` rationale below. For P1 the rationale ASSERTS atom
# identity (that is what makes the attack real); for P2 it asserts the deliberate NON-identity of
# the setting index (that is what makes the non-attack honest, not an oversight).
#
# Expected verdict is at the bottom of this file (golden oracle). Agreement map + comparability
# CQ: docs/demos/d3-agreement-map.md.

artifact pruning_cluster at sha256:cccc...
policy agreement-v1
use backends [nd@1]

# ===============================================================================================
# P1 — GENUINE DISAGREEMENT (same atoms -> contrary instance -> contested 2-cycle)
# ===============================================================================================

# --- Paper A: APT beats Prune+Distill (CoFi) at matched sparsity -------------------------------

claim c_pos
  nl      = "APT outperforms Prune+Distill (CoFi) in accuracy on RoBERTa-base MNLI at 60% sparsity"
  formal  = better(apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60)
  binding = { author = alice, audit-status = reviewed,
              rationale = "A3: same (apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60) atoms as c_neg; replication targets the identical system-baseline-metric-setting, verified identical under nf, so this pair is a real contrary instance" }

leaf a_e1 : reports(paperA_exp1, compares(apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60, favorable))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperA/table_2.csv#row=mnli-60]

leaf a_e2 : matched_setup(apt, cofi, paperA_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperA.pdf#sec=4-setup]

leaf a_e3 : valid_measurement(accuracy, paperA_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperA.pdf#sec=4-metrics]

leaf a_e4 : variance_reported(paperA_exp1)
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperA/table_2.csv#col=stddev]

leaf a_e5 : scope_matched(apt, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperA.pdf#sec=4-scope]

arg pa : supports(c_pos) by controlled_comparison(apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60, paperA_exp1)
  discharge baseline_parity      with a_e2
  discharge measurement_validity with a_e3
  discharge variance_reported    with a_e4
  discharge scope_match          with a_e5

# --- Paper B: an independent replication finds no advantage at the SAME setting -----------------

claim c_neg
  nl      = "An independent replication finds APT is not better than Prune+Distill (CoFi) in accuracy on RoBERTa-base MNLI at 60% sparsity"
  formal  = not_better(apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60)
  binding = { author = bob, audit-status = reviewed,
              rationale = "A3: SAME (apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60) as c_pos -- same system, baseline, accuracy metric, RoBERTa/MNLI, and 60% sparsity; a failed replication of paperA, not a different measurement" }

leaf b_e1 : reports(paperB_exp1, compares(apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60, null))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperB/table_1.csv#row=mnli-60]

leaf b_e2 : matched_setup(apt, cofi, paperB_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperB.pdf#sec=3-protocol]

leaf b_e3 : valid_measurement(accuracy, paperB_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperB.pdf#sec=3-metrics]

leaf b_e4 : powered(paperB_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperB.pdf#sec=3-power]

arg pb : supports(c_neg) by null_comparison(apt, cofi, accuracy, roberta_mnli_s60, paperB_exp1)
  discharge baseline_parity      with b_e2
  discharge measurement_validity with b_e3
  discharge adequate_power       with b_e4

# Mutual rebuttal: pa concludes better(...), pb concludes not_better(...) on the SAME atoms, and
# both orientations are declared contrary in agreement-v1. Neither paper is preferred (v0.1 has no
# preference ordering), so neither defeat is one-sided: a 2-cycle. Attack completeness (spec §8)
# requires BOTH edges once both sides of the contrary instance are present.
rebut pb pa    # paper B rebuts paper A's positive claim
rebut pa pb    # paper A rebuts paper B's null claim

# ===============================================================================================
# P2 — SETTING MISMATCH (same S/B/Q, different setting index D -> no contrary instance -> no edge)
# ===============================================================================================

# --- Paper C: magnitude pruning is near-lossless (BERT/GLUE, 50% sparsity) ----------------------

claim c_low
  nl      = "Magnitude pruning matches the dense baseline in accuracy on BERT GLUE at 50% sparsity (pruning is near-lossless)"
  formal  = better(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, accuracy, bert_glue_s50)
  binding = { author = carol, audit-status = reviewed,
              rationale = "A3: setting index D = bert_glue_s50 is DELIBERATELY distinct from c_high llama_openllm_s90; same system-baseline-metric, so the ONLY non-matching atom is the setting -- the prose disagreement with c_high is not an atom-level conflict" }

leaf c_e1 : reports(paperC_exp1, compares(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, accuracy, bert_glue_s50, favorable))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperC/table_3.csv#row=glue-50]

leaf c_e2 : matched_setup(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, paperC_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperC.pdf#sec=5-setup]

leaf c_e3 : valid_measurement(accuracy, paperC_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperC.pdf#sec=5-metrics]

leaf c_e4 : variance_reported(paperC_exp1)
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperC/table_3.csv#col=stddev]

leaf c_e5 : scope_matched(magnitude_pruning, accuracy, bert_glue_s50)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperC.pdf#sec=5-scope]

arg pc : supports(c_low) by controlled_comparison(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, accuracy, bert_glue_s50, paperC_exp1)
  discharge baseline_parity      with c_e2
  discharge measurement_validity with c_e3
  discharge variance_reported    with c_e4
  discharge scope_match          with c_e5

# --- Paper D: magnitude pruning destroys accuracy (LLaMA2-7B/OpenLLM, 90% sparsity) -------------

claim c_high
  nl      = "Magnitude pruning is not better than the dense baseline in accuracy on LLaMA2-7B OpenLLM at 90% sparsity (pruning is destructive)"
  formal  = not_better(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, accuracy, llama_openllm_s90)
  binding = { author = dave, audit-status = reviewed,
              rationale = "A3: setting index D = llama_openllm_s90 is DELIBERATELY distinct from c_low bert_glue_s50; the prose reads as a contradiction of c_low but the pair shares no atom on D, so no contrary instance is formed" }

leaf d_e1 : reports(paperD_exp1, compares(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, accuracy, llama_openllm_s90, null))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperD/table_1.csv#row=openllm-90]

leaf d_e2 : matched_setup(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, paperD_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperD.pdf#sec=2-setup]

leaf d_e3 : valid_measurement(accuracy, paperD_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperD.pdf#sec=2-metrics]

leaf d_e4 : powered(paperD_exp1)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [paperD.pdf#sec=2-power]

arg pd : supports(c_high) by null_comparison(magnitude_pruning, dense_baseline, accuracy, llama_openllm_s90, paperD_exp1)
  discharge baseline_parity      with d_e2
  discharge measurement_validity with d_e3
  discharge adequate_power       with d_e4

# NO attack between pc and pd: better(mp, dense, accuracy, bert_glue_s50) and
# not_better(mp, dense, accuracy, llama_openllm_s90) do NOT unify with either orientation of the
# contrary pattern (the pattern requires the SAME D on both sides; here D differs). Attack
# completeness forces no edge because there is no contrary instance to complete. This is the
# demonstrated feature — see the comparability CQ in docs/demos/d3-agreement-map.md.

status c_pos
status c_neg
status c_low
status c_high

# ==============================================================================================
# EXPECTED VERDICT (golden oracle)
# ==============================================================================================
# Compiled AF (args in declaration order pa=0, pb=1, pc=2, pd=3; all complete):
#   pa <-> pb    (a 2-cycle: rebut pb pa, rebut pa pb)
#   pc           (unattacked)
#   pd           (unattacked)
# Grounded labelling:
#   pa -> undec  (2-cycle with pb; neither in, neither out — spec §8, assumption A5)
#   pb -> undec  (2-cycle with pa)
#   pc -> in     (unattacked)
#   pd -> in     (unattacked)
# Claim status (spec §8 aggregation):
#   c_pos  -> contested   (its support pa is undec, none in)   } P1 genuine disagreement
#   c_neg  -> contested   (its support pb is undec, none in)   }
#   c_low  -> justified   (its support pc is in)               } P2 setting mismatch:
#   c_high -> justified   (its support pd is in)               } ZERO attacks, NOT contested
#
# The agreement map, in one graph:
#   - P1's two papers ATTACK each other (contested x2) because they share the same atoms.
#   - P2's two papers do NOT attack (justified x2) because their setting index D differs — the
#     calculus refuses to manufacture a disagreement the atoms do not license (a silent
#     false-independence made explicit).
#
# §8.1 note: the contested propositions here (better/not_better on roberta_mnli_s60) are the
# HEADLINE claims, exactly the condition README.md flags for a Path-B well-formedness collision.
# No collision occurs: both are conclusions of DEFEASIBLE schemes only (no strict rule, no strict
# chain touches them), so agreement-v1 passes the §8.1 R12 check and no flip to Path A is forced.
# Recorded, not routed around — see docs/demos/d3-agreement-map.md.
#
# Teaching points:
#  1. Corpus scale needs no new calculus (spec §8): P1 is example B's shape at the pruning
#     cluster's real grain; adding papers is adding arguments + edges to the same AF.
#  2. `contested` (not "both justified") is the honest verdict for an unresolved same-setting
#     field disagreement, and it falls out of grounded semantics.
#  3. Atom identity is load-bearing (A3): P1 and P2 have IDENTICAL prose-level "disagreement"
#     structure; only the setting index D differs, and that single difference flips the verdict
#     from contested x2 to justified x2. The open comparability CQ names what evidence would
#     connect P2's papers — a shared-setting bridging experiment — since v0.1 has no cross-setting
#     contrary to link them (the possible-worlds follow-up, out of scope here).
